{id:'hindustan-unilever',name:'Hindustan Unilever',ticker:'HINDUNILVR',sector:'Everyday essentials',initial:'H',color:'#164d92',bg:'#eaf2ff',tag:'Start with what you know',summary:'The business behind many of the products in your kitchen, bathroom and laundry.',description:'Hindustan Unilever sells packaged consumer goods in India. People buy its products through shops and online retailers, which are supplied through its distribution network.',products:[['Surf excel','Laundry care'],['Dove','Personal care'],['Vim','Household cleaning'],['Horlicks','Nutrition']],money:'It earns revenue from selling consumer goods. What matters is how many units it sells, the prices it can charge and the cost of making and distributing them.',ownership:'This profile covers the Indian listed company, Hindustan Unilever Limited. Do not treat every product sold by the global Unilever group as part of HUL.',watch:[['Volume growth','Are people buying more units, rather than simply paying higher prices?'],['Operating margin','How much operating profit remains from each rupee of sales?'],['Product mix','Are higher-value categories becoming a bigger part of sales?']],risks:[['Input costs','Costlier ingredients and packaging can reduce profit unless prices or efficiency improve.'],['Competition','Rival brands and local alternatives can compete on price, shelf space and advertising.'],['Demand','Household budgets influence what consumers buy and how often.']],sources:[['HUL company website','https://www.hul.co.in/'],['FY 2025–26 annual report','https://www.hul.co.in/files/annual-report-2025-26.pdf']],update:['FY 2025–26 report available','The annual report is a starting point for checking the current business structure and financial performance.'],question:'Why can revenue rise even when the number of products sold does not?',answer:'Revenue can rise because of higher prices or a shift toward more expensive products. That is why volume growth deserves a separate look.'},
{id:'tcs',name:'Tata Consultancy Services',ticker:'TCS',sector:'Technology & services',initial:'tcs',color:'#7340a9',bg:'#f2edfc',tag:'Beyond physical products',summary:'The technology partner helping large organisations run and change their businesses.',description:'TCS provides technology services, consulting and business solutions to organisations across industries and countries. Its customers are businesses rather than individual shoppers.',products:[['IT services','Build and maintain systems'],['Consulting','Plan business changes'],['Cloud services','Modernise technology'],['Business solutions','Support enterprise operations']],money:'Clients pay for technology projects and ongoing services. Contract pricing, employee costs and how efficiently teams deliver work all influence profit.',ownership:'Tata Consultancy Services Limited is the listed business covered here. Other Tata group companies are separate businesses; buying TCS shares does not give you direct ownership of the entire Tata group.',watch:[['Revenue growth','Are clients spending more on services, after considering currency changes?'],['Operating margin','How well does pricing cover delivery costs?'],['Order book','New contracts can indicate demand, but are not all immediate revenue.']],risks:[['Client spending','Customers can postpone technology projects when their own outlook weakens.'],['Delivery costs','Employee and subcontracting costs affect profitability.'],['Technology shifts','Automation changes what clients buy and the skills needed to serve them.']],sources:[['FY 2025–26 annual report','https://www.tcs.com/content/dam/tcs/investor-relations/financial-statements/2025-26/ar/annual-report-2025-2026.pdf']],update:['FY 2025–26 report available','Use the report to compare industry exposure and results across reporting periods.'],question:'Does a newly announced contract become revenue immediately?',answer:'Usually not in full. Revenue is recognised as the company delivers work under the contract; an order book is different from sales already earned.'},
{id:'reliance-industries',name:'Reliance Industries',ticker:'RELIANCE',sector:'Diversified business',initial:'R',color:'#987423',bg:'#faf2de',tag:'One company, many businesses',summary:'A group connecting energy and materials with retail and digital services.',description:'Reliance Industries spans energy and materials, digital services, retail and other businesses. Understanding it means looking at the different activities within the group.',products:[['Jio','Digital services'],['Reliance Retail','Retail businesses'],['Fuels','Energy products'],['Petrochemicals','Industrial materials']],money:'Different divisions earn money in different ways: selling fuels and materials, selling retail goods and providing digital services. Group revenue alone does not explain which divisions drive profit.',ownership:'This profile covers Reliance Industries Limited and its group businesses. Jio and retail operations sit within group entities. Other companies with “Reliance” in their names are not automatically part of this listed business.',watch:[['Segment earnings','Which businesses contribute operating earnings, and how is that changing?'],['Capital spending','How much is being invested before projects generate cash?'],['Debt and cash flow','Can cash generation support investment and obligations?']],risks:[['Commodity cycles','Energy and chemical profitability can change with global demand and pricing.'],['Large investments','New projects can take time to generate returns.'],['Different competitors','Retail and digital services face competition distinct from the energy business.']],sources:[['FY 2025–26 business overview','https://www.ril.com/ar2025-26/index.html'],['Management discussion','https://www.ril.com/ar2025-26/mda.html']],update:['FY 2025–26 report available','Read each business discussion separately before drawing conclusions from the group total.'],question:'Must the division with the most sales also generate the most profit?',answer:'No. Divisions have different margins and costs. Revenue share and profit contribution answer different questions.'}
